#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079402} :activity/id #uuid "b34e1933-3814-529f-88c0-52e2e04c4df5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391 :topic/id #uuid "9b93c13d-3e57-5a78-b8a6-0f0a4d64062e" :topic/math-academy-id 614} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079670 :tutorial/content "A second-order, linear, homogeneous ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients has the general form\n\n$$\na\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + b\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + cy =0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nTo find solutions, we can propose a trial solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ where $\\lambda$ is a constant to be determined. Differentiating this gives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the general ODE, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a({\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x})+b(\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x})+c({e}^{\\lambda x}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}(a{\\lambda }^{2}+b\\lambda +c) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{\\lambda x}$ can never be zero, the expression in the parentheses must be zero:\n\n$$\na\\lambda^2+b\\lambda + c =0 .\n$$\n\nThis is called the **characteristic equation** (or **auxiliary equation**). The roots of this quadratic equation for $\\lambda$ give us the solutions to the original differential equation.\n\nNext, we'll use the characteristic equation to solve a specific example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8c3fdb1-a293-55b7-bb3f-a3b91cd13c06" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4046 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "70588abd-28cf-57e8-91ad-d7a423e0a722" :step/math-academy-id 24219 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079553}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078494 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078337 :question/id #uuid "c47d5c55-80f0-5093-810b-bf9c718c5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421926}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "8acd33b3-bbd7-5f1f-ae06-547799b4f66d" :step/math-academy-id 24220 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079404}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078338 :question/id #uuid "2065790f-428d-5477-ac77-0c5d07853143" :question/math-academy-id "e-6588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050522}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421928}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "3590f636-f3a1-5592-a64c-2fb248cb17db" :step/math-academy-id 24221 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079405}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078339 :question/id #uuid "1c7b0878-a386-5449-9ff4-c37c8cda1b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427572}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "4f98b762-c792-57e5-8e73-6917892dea16" :step/math-academy-id 24222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079617 :tutorial/content "Let's solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2 y}{\\mathrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} + 6y =0\n$$\n\nFrom the previous slide, we know this ODE has a characteristic equation of the form $a\\lambda^2 + b\\lambda + c=0.$ Here, $a=1, b=5,$ and $c=6,$ so the characteristic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots are $\\lambda_1 = -2$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3.$ Each root gives a particular solution to the ODE: $y_1=e^{-2x}$ and $y_2=e^{-3x}.$\n\nThe **general solution** is a linear combination of these particular solutions:\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe48b08c-e63b-577c-ba34-bc61bc5b2966" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14884 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "88086b0a-3e17-59ee-bc49-6709f897fb53" :step/math-academy-id 607934 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079403}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078644}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427573} :activity/id #uuid "227e8665-3923-5e3b-9f94-01de1d340546" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391 :topic/id #uuid "9b93c13d-3e57-5a78-b8a6-0f0a4d64062e" :topic/math-academy-id 614} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bec9d70-f8de-51cf-9e64-a849b809ce13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342607} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0ed665-7b86-5adb-85fc-2c91f4201111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343490} {:answer/id #uuid "c479827f-1f07-57f4-80cf-2fa9049345a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{6x}+B{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343966} {:answer/id #uuid "c65f01c2-eff5-56e3-8d94-e7a8e6fe2b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343978} {:answer/id #uuid "f51f0ae1-02ed-5b8f-a1ff-80e0e5ce8f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342607} :answer-field/id #uuid "c56404db-2432-58f9-a3d9-cc82f8d56420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4af7b02-aa3b-5c89-83b9-f0034ff7803a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7781" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +4)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-4.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c217041-6e07-5b2b-ab0b-44e2beb8265c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1ea764-4dca-5820-9034-d4b4f70f730b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355221} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae4ce25-9e97-5331-8ed8-96b2d9024430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355466} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba0b662-5fc6-5644-94c1-0e8b93d1e722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355900} {:answer/id #uuid "aff3c5d4-9583-5b46-a41e-d92a6fb9fbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356214} {:answer/id #uuid "d0936d80-a410-5f00-b078-134eaae76756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355900} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf81e32-4f57-597a-af29-291088b46e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb0ac50b-6a2d-5d53-997b-cb65e0ad2bb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-90720" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =1$ or $\\lambda =6.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "90c0dd24-f5ff-5d53-a9db-2ea11550684f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bda799f-2dc1-508a-b332-a2dc15ad6469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{-5t}-4{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427566} {:answer/id #uuid "463b0c74-3f5f-5ede-984f-20e81cf9dab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-5t}+6{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427567} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3822e6-4ea3-5bd8-88d6-1f35c1d51f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-5t}+2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427568} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd12f7d-a1de-5498-8a07-57a3b71dfca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427569} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c2bf7c-2f4c-5e0e-8a59-5fd6f0e6f6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427568} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1bdcac-c8f7-5ea7-bb80-b176a1307c71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "128c4108-f47c-515a-aaac-ed93eb92e39c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64708" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-10y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-10{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-5$ or $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-5t}$ and ${e}^{2t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $-3{e}^{-5t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ is not a solution."} :step/id #uuid "b72723e4-6cca-52eb-af62-fa6496045372" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427574}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427565}]}
